    content: `Food travels through a single continuous muscular tube — the "alimentary canal" or "gastrointestinal (GI) tract" — about 9 metres long from end to end. THE ROUTE: (1) MOUTH — teeth chew (mechanical), salivary glands add saliva (lubrication + salivary amylase starts on starch), and the tongue forms a "bolus" and pushes it back. (2) PHARYNX and (3) OESOPHAGUS — swallowing (deglutition) reflexly pushes the bolus into the oesophagus, which carries it down to the stomach by waves of muscle contraction (peristalsis), through the lower oesophageal sphincter. (4) STOMACH — a J-shaped bag that stores the meal, churns it with muscular contractions, bathes it in hydrochloric acid and the enzyme pepsin, kills most swallowed microbes, and turns it into a semi-liquid "chyme," which it then releases in controlled squirts through the pyloric sphincter. (5) SMALL INTESTINE — the ~6–7 m long site of most digestion and virtually all nutrient absorption, in three parts: the DUODENUM (receives bile from the gallbladder/liver and enzyme-rich alkaline juice from the pancreas, neutralising the acid), the JEJUNUM (bulk absorption), and the ILEUM (absorbs vitamin B12 and bile salts). Its lining is folded and covered in finger-like villi and microvilli for a huge absorptive surface. (6) LARGE INTESTINE (colon) — caecum → ascending → transverse → descending → sigmoid; it reabsorbs most of the remaining water and electrolytes, and its ~100 trillion resident bacteria ferment undigested fibre (making short-chain fatty acids and some vitamins), compacting the residue into faeces. (7) RECTUM — stores the faeces until defecation. (8) ANUS — the internal (involuntary) and external (voluntary) sphincters control elimination. ACCESSORY ORGANS — the salivary glands, LIVER (makes bile), GALLBLADDER (stores and concentrates bile), and PANCREAS (makes digestive enzymes + bicarbonate) all pour secretions INTO the tract but food never passes through them. Total transit time is typically 24–72 hours, mostly determined by how long material spends in the colon.`,
