    content: `THE STOMACH'S JOBS: (1) STORAGE — it stretches to hold a whole meal (roughly 1–1.5 L, up to ~4 L) so you can eat faster than you can digest. (2) MECHANICAL DIGESTION — three muscle layers churn, knead, and grind the food. (3) CHEMICAL DIGESTION — "parietal cells" secrete hydrochloric acid (HCl), which denatures proteins and activates pepsin; "chief cells" secrete pepsinogen, which the acid converts to PEPSIN, the enzyme that begins breaking proteins into peptides. (4) DEFENCE — the strong acid (pH ~1.5–2) kills most bacteria, viruses, and parasites in food. (5) VITAMIN B12 — parietal cells also make "intrinsic factor," a protein essential for absorbing vitamin B12 later in the ileum (its loss causes pernicious anaemia). (6) CONTROLLED EMPTYING — it releases chyme into the duodenum in small amounts matched to the small intestine's capacity, and it secretes the hormone gastrin. WHY IT DOESN'T DIGEST ITSELF: the stomach lining is protected by the "gastric mucosal barrier": (a) surface mucous cells secrete a thick, adherent gel of MUCUS mixed with BICARBONATE, so that even though the lumen is at pH 2, the surface of the cells sits at nearly pH 7 — the mucus traps a neutralising layer against the wall; (b) the epithelial cells are sealed together by tight junctions that stop acid seeping between them; (c) pepsin is made and stored in the inactive "zymogen" form (pepsinogen) so it can't digest the cells that produce it; (d) prostaglandins (locally made) keep the protective mucus, bicarbonate, and blood flow high, and stimulate rapid replacement of surface cells (the whole lining renews every 3–6 days). Peptic ulcers occur when this balance tips toward the aggressive side — chiefly from Helicobacter pylori infection (chronic inflammation weakens the barrier) and from NSAID painkillers (aspirin, ibuprofen) which block the protective prostaglandins — with alcohol, smoking, and severe physiological stress as additional factors.`,
