    content: `Pepsin is the principal protein-digesting enzyme secreted by the stomach. It is an "endopeptidase" — it cleaves proteins internally, at bonds next to aromatic and other hydrophobic amino acids, chopping large dietary proteins into shorter polypeptides and peptides (it does not finish the job to free amino acids; that happens later in the small intestine). Pepsin is UNUSUAL among enzymes in that its optimum pH is extremely acidic — around 1.5 to 2 — and it is denatured and inactivated by neutral or alkaline conditions. ACTIVATION: pepsin is synthesised and secreted by the "chief cells" (peptic cells) in the gastric glands as an INACTIVE precursor, PEPSINOGEN — a "zymogen" (proenzyme). It is made inactive so that it cannot digest the very cells and glands that produce and store it. Once pepsinogen is secreted into the acidic stomach lumen, two things activate it: (1) the low pH (created by hydrochloric acid from the neighbouring parietal cells) causes pepsinogen to unfold and auto-cleave, removing a ~44-amino-acid "propeptide" that was blocking the active site, exposing active PEPSIN; (2) pepsin, once formed, then rapidly activates more pepsinogen molecules — a self-amplifying (autocatalytic) cascade. Pepsin secretion and acid secretion are stimulated together by the sight/smell/taste of food (vagal), by gastrin, and by stomach distension. When the chyme passes into the duodenum, the pancreatic bicarbonate neutralises the acid, the pH rises, and pepsin is irreversibly inactivated — protein digestion is then continued by the pancreatic proteases (trypsin, chymotrypsin, elastase, carboxypeptidases), which are themselves secreted as zymogens and activated in the intestinal lumen. (Pepsin is a human digestive enzyme; it is not "rennet," which is the enzyme mixture — mainly chymosin — from a calf's stomach used to curdle milk for cheese.)`,
