    content: `Bile is a bitter, greenish-yellow to golden-brown fluid produced continuously by the liver's hepatocytes (about 0.5–1 L per day) and delivered — either directly, or via the gallbladder where it is stored and concentrated between meals — into the duodenum. IMPORTANT: bile contains NO digestive enzymes. Its constituents are: BILE SALTS (bile acids conjugated with glycine or taurine — synthesised by the liver from cholesterol; the functional component), PHOSPHOLIPIDS (mainly lecithin), CHOLESTEROL, the waste pigment BILIRUBIN (a yellow-brown breakdown product of the haem from old red blood cells — this is what colours bile, and, converted by gut bacteria to stercobilin, colours faeces brown), electrolytes, water, and bicarbonate. WHAT BILE DOES: (1) EMULSIFICATION OF FAT — bile salts are "amphipathic": one face of the molecule is water-loving (hydrophilic) and the other is fat-loving (hydrophobic). They coat large globules of dietary fat and, with the mechanical churning of the intestine, break them into a fine suspension of tiny droplets. This does not chemically digest the fat, but it multiplies the surface area available to pancreatic LIPASE (the enzyme that actually splits the triglycerides) many thousand-fold, without which fat digestion would be far too slow. (2) MICELLE FORMATION AND ABSORPTION — once lipase has released free fatty acids and monoglycerides, bile salts assemble them (together with cholesterol and the fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K) into microscopic spherical carriers called MICELLES, which ferry these poorly water-soluble products through the watery intestinal contents up to the brush border of the enterocytes, where they are absorbed. (3) EXCRETION — bile is the main pathway by which the body gets rid of excess cholesterol, of bilirubin, and of many drugs, hormone metabolites, and heavy metals (copper), which leave the body in the stool. (4) It also has bacteriostatic properties and helps neutralise stomach acid. RECYCLING: about 95% of the bile salts are reabsorbed in the terminal ileum and returned to the liver via the portal vein to be re-secreted — the "enterohepatic circulation" — cycling roughly 6 times per meal, so the small total bile-salt pool (~3 g) is enough. Loss of this recycling (ileal disease or resection) causes bile-salt diarrhoea and fat malabsorption. When bile becomes supersaturated with cholesterol, gallstones form.`,
