    content: `The gallbladder is a small (about 7–10 cm, holding ~30–50 mL when full), pear-shaped, muscular sac lying in a shallow bed on the underside of the liver's right lobe, connected to the bile duct system by the cystic duct. Its function is purely storage and concentration — it does NOT make bile (the liver does), and it contains NO enzymes. HOW IT WORKS: the liver secretes bile continuously, but between meals there is nothing in the duodenum for the bile to act on, so a valve (the sphincter of Oddi) at the duct's outlet stays closed and bile backs up and is diverted into the gallbladder. There the gallbladder wall actively absorbs water, sodium, and chloride out of the bile, concentrating it 5- to 20-fold (this also makes it a place gallstones tend to form). When a meal — especially one containing fat or protein — arrives in the duodenum, enteroendocrine cells there release the hormone CHOLECYSTOKININ (CCK) into the blood. CCK causes the smooth muscle of the gallbladder wall to contract and, simultaneously, the sphincter of Oddi to relax, so a bolus of concentrated bile is ejected down the cystic duct and common bile duct into the duodenum, arriving just when the fat is there to be emulsified. (Vagal nerve stimulation during the "cephalic phase" of a meal also contributes.) LIVING WITHOUT ONE: the gallbladder is not essential. It is removed ("cholecystectomy," usually keyhole) when it causes trouble — gallstones blocking the cystic duct (biliary colic), inflammation (cholecystitis), or a stone passing into the common bile duct. After removal, bile simply flows continuously from the liver into the gut; most people notice nothing, though some have looser or more frequent stools and mild difficulty with very fatty meals for a while.`,
