    content: `The wall of the gastrointestinal tract, from the oesophagus to the anal canal, is built from FOUR concentric tissue layers (tunics). Listed from the inside (the lumen, where the food is) outward: (1) MUCOSA — the innermost layer and the one in contact with the food. It has three sub-parts: an EPITHELIUM (the actual lining cells — stratified squamous for protection in the mouth, oesophagus, and anus; simple columnar for secretion and absorption in the stomach and intestines, thrown into villi in the small intestine); the LAMINA PROPRIA (a bed of loose connective tissue underneath, packed with capillaries, lymphatic vessels — the "lacteals" of the villi — and immune tissue, the MALT/GALT including Peyer's patches); and the MUSCULARIS MUCOSAE (a very thin layer of smooth muscle that twitches the mucosa to keep the surface in contact with contents). (2) SUBMUCOSA — a thicker layer of dense, irregular connective tissue containing the LARGER blood vessels and lymphatics, some glands (in the oesophagus and duodenum), and the SUBMUCOSAL (Meissner's) nerve plexus, which controls glandular secretion and local blood flow. (3) MUSCULARIS EXTERNA (muscularis propria) — the main muscle coat, normally two layers of smooth muscle: an INNER CIRCULAR layer (squeezes the lumen shut) and an OUTER LONGITUDINAL layer (shortens the segment). The stomach adds a third, innermost oblique layer for churning; the oesophagus is skeletal muscle in its upper third. Between the two layers lies the MYENTERIC (Auerbach's) nerve plexus, which coordinates PERISTALSIS (propulsive waves) and SEGMENTATION (mixing contractions). Sphincters are thickened rings of the circular layer. (4) SEROSA (visceral peritoneum) — a thin outer layer of connective tissue covered by a slippery single sheet of mesothelium, present where the organ hangs free inside the peritoneal cavity; where the organ is fixed to the body wall (much of the oesophagus, the rectum, parts of the duodenum and colon), this outer layer is instead called the ADVENTITIA (fibrous connective tissue merging with surrounding structures). (This is a four-layer tube — it has nothing to do with the single layer of endothelial cells that forms a capillary.)`,
