    content: `A peptic ulcer is an erosion in the wall of the upper GI tract that penetrates deeper than the muscularis mucosae — most often in the first part of the DUODENUM (duodenal ulcer, the commonest, usually a young-to-middle-aged patient) or the STOMACH (gastric ulcer, older patients, and which must always be biopsied to exclude cancer); occasionally in the lower oesophagus or a stoma. THE BASIC PROBLEM: the mucosa is normally in balance between "aggressive" factors (hydrochloric acid, pepsin) and "defensive" factors (the mucus–bicarbonate gel layer, tight epithelial junctions, good mucosal blood flow, prostaglandins, rapid cell turnover). An ulcer forms when this balance tips. THE TWO MAJOR CAUSES: (1) HELICOBACTER PYLORI — a Gram-negative, spiral, highly motile (flagellated) bacterium uniquely adapted to live in the stomach: it burrows into the protective mucus layer and shelters against the epithelium, and it produces the enzyme urease, which splits urea into ammonia to buffer the acid around it. It provokes a chronic active gastritis that impairs the mucosal defences and disturbs acid regulation, and it is responsible for roughly 90% of duodenal ulcers and 70–80% of gastric ulcers. H. pylori is also a WHO class-1 (definite) carcinogen — a cause of gastric adenocarcinoma and of gastric MALT lymphoma — though most infected people never get an ulcer or cancer. (2) NSAIDs — aspirin, ibuprofen, naproxen, diclofenac and the rest inhibit cyclo-oxygenase and therefore the synthesis of the prostaglandins that keep the mucus, bicarbonate, and blood flow high; they cause gastric ulcers especially, and are the leading cause of ulcers in people who are H. pylori-negative. Other causes: physiological stress ulcers in critically ill patients (Curling's ulcer after burns, Cushing's ulcer after head injury), smoking, and the rare Zollinger–Ellison syndrome (a gastrin-secreting tumour driving massive acid output). THE HISTORY: for most of the 20th century ulcers were blamed on stress, personality, and spicy food; in 1982 Barry Marshall and Robin Warren identified H. pylori, and Marshall famously drank a culture of it, developed gastritis, and cured himself with antibiotics — work that won the 2005 Nobel Prize. SYMPTOMS: burning or gnawing epigastric pain (classically relieved by eating in duodenal ulcers, worsened by eating in gastric ulcers), nocturnal pain, nausea, and early satiety. COMPLICATIONS (potentially fatal): bleeding (vomiting blood, or black tarry stools), perforation (sudden severe pain, peritonitis), and gastric outlet obstruction from scarring. TREATMENT: a proton-pump inhibitor to suppress acid and let the ulcer heal, "eradication therapy" (a PPI plus two antibiotics for 1–2 weeks) if H. pylori is present, and stopping NSAIDs (or co-prescribing a PPI if they're essential).`,
