    content: `IBS and IBD sound alike and share symptoms (abdominal pain, altered bowel habit, bloating) but are fundamentally different kinds of problem. IBS — IRRITABLE BOWEL SYNDROME — is a FUNCTIONAL gastrointestinal disorder, now formally termed a "disorder of gut–brain interaction." The bowel is structurally NORMAL: there is no inflammation, no ulceration, no tissue damage, blood tests and endoscopy are normal, it does not progress to cancer, and it does not shorten life. What is abnormal is how the gut FUNCTIONS and how its signals are processed: visceral hypersensitivity (normal gut sensations are perceived as painful), altered motility (too fast or too slow), altered gut microbiome and bile-acid handling, low-grade immune activation, and a strong bidirectional link with stress, anxiety, and depression via the gut–brain axis (which is why symptoms flare with stress). It is very common (~10% of adults, more women), diagnosed clinically using the Rome IV criteria (recurrent abdominal pain, on average ≥1 day/week, associated with defecation and/or a change in stool frequency or form) after excluding warning features, and sub-typed as IBS-D (diarrhoea), IBS-C (constipation), or IBS-M (mixed). Management: dietary change (a trial of the low-FODMAP diet, soluble fibre, limiting caffeine/alcohol/fatty foods), antispasmodics, targeted laxatives or antidiarrhoeals, low-dose neuromodulators (tricyclics, SSRIs), peppermint oil, probiotics, and psychological therapies (CBT, gut-directed hypnotherapy). IBD — INFLAMMATORY BOWEL DISEASE — means CROHN'S DISEASE and ULCERATIVE COLITIS, which are ORGANIC (structural), immune-mediated diseases with genuine, chronic, relapsing INFLAMMATION and ULCERATION of the bowel wall. The damage is visible on colonoscopy and confirmed on biopsy; inflammatory markers (CRP, faecal calprotectin) are raised; and untreated it leads to strictures, fistulas and abscesses (Crohn's), toxic megacolon and an increased colorectal cancer risk (ulcerative colitis), malnutrition, and often surgery. It is less common (~0.5–1%), tends to present younger, and is treated with anti-inflammatory drugs (5-ASA), corticosteroids for flares, immunosuppressants (azathioprine, methotrexate), and biologic/targeted drugs (anti-TNF, anti-integrin, anti-IL-23, JAK inhibitors), plus surgery. THE SHORT ANSWER: IBS = symptoms without any detectable damage; IBD = symptoms with real inflammation and damage. "Red flag" features that argue against simple IBS and mandate investigation for IBD (or cancer): rectal bleeding, unintentional weight loss, iron-deficiency anaemia, fever, symptoms that wake the patient at night, a first onset over age 50, and a family history of IBD or bowel/ovarian cancer.`,
