    content: `The pancreas, a soft gland ~15 cm long lying behind the stomach, is really two organs fused together, doing entirely separate jobs. THE EXOCRINE PANCREAS makes up about 98% of the organ's mass. It consists of grape-like clusters of secretory "acinar cells" draining into an ever-branching network of ducts that unite into the main pancreatic duct, which joins the common bile duct and empties into the duodenum at the ampulla of Vater. It secretes ~1–1.5 litres per day of "pancreatic juice" with two components: (1) DIGESTIVE ENZYMES from the acinar cells — pancreatic amylase (starch → maltose), pancreatic lipase with colipase (triglycerides → fatty acids + monoglycerides — the body's main fat-digesting enzyme), and the protein-digesting enzymes secreted as inactive zymogens (trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, proelastase, procarboxypeptidases) which are activated only once in the duodenum (enteropeptidase on the gut wall converts trypsinogen to trypsin, which then activates the rest); plus nucleases. (2) BICARBONATE from the duct cells — a large alkaline flood, triggered by the hormone SECRETIN when acid enters the duodenum, that neutralises the stomach acid so the intestinal enzymes (which need a near-neutral pH) can work and so the duodenal lining isn't burned. THE ENDOCRINE PANCREAS is only ~1–2% of the mass: about one to two million tiny cell clusters — the ISLETS OF LANGERHANS — embedded throughout the exocrine tissue, richly supplied with capillaries, secreting hormones DIRECTLY INTO THE BLOOD (no duct). The main islet cells: BETA cells (~60–70%) make INSULIN, which lowers blood glucose after a meal; ALPHA cells (~20%) make GLUCAGON, which raises blood glucose during fasting; DELTA cells make SOMATOSTATIN, which inhibits the secretion of both (and of GI hormones); PP cells make pancreatic polypeptide; and epsilon cells make ghrelin. DISEASES map onto the two roles: EXOCRINE insufficiency (chronic pancreatitis, cystic fibrosis, pancreatic cancer obstructing the duct, or after pancreatic surgery) causes maldigestion — greasy, floating, foul stools (steatorrhoea), weight loss, and fat-soluble vitamin deficiency, treated with oral enzyme capsules taken with meals. ENDOCRINE failure — loss or dysfunction of the beta cells — causes DIABETES MELLITUS (type 1: autoimmune beta-cell destruction; type 2: insulin resistance plus progressive beta-cell exhaustion). ACUTE PANCREATITIS (usually from gallstones or alcohol) is the emergency where the digestive zymogens activate prematurely INSIDE the gland and it begins to digest itself.`,
