    content: `The large intestine (colon) is about 1.5 metres long and runs a picture-frame shape around the border of the abdomen. Its segments, in order of flow: (1) CAECUM — a blind-ended pouch in the right lower abdomen where the ileum of the small intestine empties in through the ileocaecal valve; the vermiform APPENDIX hangs from its lower end. (2) ASCENDING COLON — runs straight up the right side of the abdomen. (3) HEPATIC (right colic) FLEXURE — the sharp bend under the right lobe of the liver. (4) TRANSVERSE COLON — crosses the upper abdomen from right to left (the most mobile part, slung on the transverse mesocolon). (5) SPLENIC (left colic) FLEXURE — the bend near the spleen, higher and more acute than the hepatic flexure. (6) DESCENDING COLON — runs down the left side of the abdomen. (7) SIGMOID COLON — an S-shaped loop in the left lower abdomen and pelvis (a common site of diverticular disease, volvulus, and cancer). (8) RECTUM — the ~12–15 cm straight terminal segment in the pelvis, which stores faeces. (9) ANAL CANAL — the last ~4 cm, ending at the anus, with the internal (involuntary smooth muscle) and external (voluntary skeletal muscle) sphincters. FEATURES of the colon distinguishing it from the small intestine on inspection: three longitudinal muscle bands ("taeniae coli") that gather the wall into pouches ("haustra"), and small fatty tags ("appendices epiploicae"). FUNCTION along its length: the right (ascending) colon absorbs most of the remaining ~1–1.5 L of water and electrolytes from the ileal effluent and is where the microbiome most actively ferments fibre into short-chain fatty acids; the transverse and left (descending) colon continue dehydrating and storing the residue, moving it along with slow "mass movements" a few times a day; the sigmoid stores formed stool; and stretch of the RECTUM triggers the defecation reflex and the conscious urge to go.`,
