    title: 'The Migrating Motor Complex',
    category: 'Digestive System',
    keywords: [
      'what is the migrating motor complex mmc housekeeper of the gut fasting motility motilin',
      'the migrating motor complex is a repeating pattern of gut muscle activity that occurs only during fasting between meals and overnight not while you are digesting a meal this has nothing to do with migrating birds flapping their wings',
      'roughly every 90 to 120 minutes a front of activity sweeps down the stomach and the whole small intestine in four phases culminating in phase three a burst of strong propagating peristaltic contractions that starts in the stomach and travels all the way to the end of the small intestine',
      'its job is housekeeping it sweeps out leftover food debris shed cells mucus and bacteria toward the colon keeping the small intestine clean and preventing bacterial overgrowth it is driven by the enteric nervous system and the hormone motilin and it is switched off the instant you eat replaced by the mixing contractions of the fed pattern a weak mmc contributes to small intestinal bacterial overgrowth sibo the loud rumbling of a hungry stomach borborygmi is largely the mmc',
    ],
    content: `The migrating motor complex (MMC, or migrating myoelectric complex) is a distinctive, cyclical pattern of electrical and mechanical activity in the stomach and small intestine that occurs ONLY in the FASTING state — between meals and, most vigorously, during sleep. (It has absolutely nothing to do with migratory birds or wing-flapping — that was a bizarre wrong answer.) THE CYCLE: about every 90–120 minutes during fasting, a "front" of motor activity begins in the stomach (or upper small intestine) and MIGRATES aborally (downstream) along the small bowel to the terminal ileum, taking 1–2 hours to traverse it, then a new cycle starts. Each cycle has four phases: Phase I — motor quiescence (little or no contraction); Phase II — irregular, intermittent contractions of increasing frequency; PHASE III — the important one: a burst of strong, regular, high-amplitude PERISTALTIC contractions at the maximum rate for that segment (3/min in the stomach, ~11–12/min in the duodenum), fully occluding the lumen and propagating steadily downward; Phase IV — a brief transition back to quiescence. FUNCTION — the MMC is nicknamed the "intestinal housekeeper" or "street sweeper": phase III sweeps the stomach and small intestine clean of residual undigested food particles, sloughed epithelial cells, mucus, secretions, and — critically — bacteria, propelling them into the colon. This keeps the small intestine's bacterial population low (the small bowel normally has far fewer bacteria than the colon). REGULATION: the MMC is generated and coordinated by the ENTERIC NERVOUS SYSTEM, and its initiation in the stomach is closely tied to cyclical surges of the hormone MOTILIN from the upper small intestine (the antibiotic erythromycin mimics motilin, which is why it can be used as a "prokinetic" to speed gastric emptying). The MMC is ABOLISHED immediately when you eat — food converts the gut to the "fed pattern" of continuous, irregular, segmenting (mixing) contractions, and the MMC does not resume until digestion and absorption of that meal are essentially complete (a few hours). CLINICAL RELEVANCE: an impaired or absent MMC (seen in diabetic autonomic neuropathy, scleroderma, chronic intestinal pseudo-obstruction, after vagotomy or some GI surgery, and with opioid use) allows bacteria to accumulate and colonise the small bowel, causing SMALL INTESTINAL BACTERIAL OVERGROWTH (SIBO) with bloating, gas, diarrhoea, and malabsorption. The loud gurgling ("borborygmi") of an empty, hungry stomach is largely the sound of the MMC's phase-III contractions moving fluid and gas through the empty bowel.`,
