    content: `Saliva is the watery fluid, about 1–1.5 litres per day, produced by three pairs of major salivary glands — the parotid (in front of each ear, purely serous/watery), the submandibular (under the jaw, mixed), and the sublingual (under the tongue, mostly mucous) — plus hundreds of minor glands scattered in the mouth. It is ~99% water, with mucins, electrolytes (including bicarbonate), enzymes, and antimicrobial proteins. ITS FUNCTIONS: (1) LUBRICATION AND BOLUS FORMATION — mucus makes the chewed food slippery and binds it into a cohesive "bolus" that can be swallowed safely, and keeps the mouth, tongue, and lips moist for speech. (2) TASTE — a substance can only stimulate the taste buds if it is dissolved, and saliva is the solvent; it also contains "gustin," involved in taste-bud development. (3) BEGINNING CARBOHYDRATE DIGESTION — the main enzyme is SALIVARY AMYLASE (historically "ptyalin"), which hydrolyses cooked starch and glycogen into the disaccharide maltose and short "dextrins." It works at the neutral-to-slightly-acidic pH of the mouth; when the bolus reaches the stomach, it keeps acting for a while inside the bolus until the acid finally penetrates and denatures it (perhaps 30–60% of dietary starch is broken down before then). There is also a small amount of LINGUAL LIPASE (which begins fat digestion and continues in the stomach — important in newborns). (4) ORAL DEFENCE — LYSOZYME (digests bacterial cell walls), LACTOFERRIN (sequesters iron from bacteria), salivary peroxidase, and secretory IgA antibodies all limit microbial growth and help protect against tooth decay and oral infection. (5) PROTECTING THE TEETH — the bicarbonate in saliva buffers and neutralises the acids produced by plaque bacteria and from acidic foods, and saliva is supersaturated with calcium and phosphate (and carries any fluoride), which it deposits back into the enamel to REMINERALISE early lesions between acid attacks; it also washes away food debris and forms the protective "pellicle" film on the tooth surface. (6) It contains growth factors (EGF, NGF) that aid wound healing, which is one reason animals lick wounds. Reduced saliva ("xerostomia" / dry mouth — from medications, radiotherapy to the head and neck, Sjögren's syndrome, dehydration, or mouth-breathing) rapidly leads to rampant tooth decay, oral thrush and infections, difficulty chewing and swallowing dry food, altered taste, and sore, cracked mucosa.`,
